CSS BACKGROUND-SIZE
==================

background-size controls
how the background image
is scaled inside an element.

It controls ONLY size,
not repetition.


DEFAULT BEHAVIOR
================
If background-size is not written,
the image keeps its original size.

background-size: auto;


AUTO
====
The image is shown
in its original width and height.

Important behavior:
- Size is unchanged
- Image repeats by default

.box {
    background-size: auto;
}

To stop repeat:
background-repeat: no-repeat;


COVER
=====
The image covers the entire element.

- No empty space
- Image may be cropped
- Most commonly used

Important behavior:
- Repeat is still active
- Usually not visible because image fills container

.box {
    background-size: cover;
}

Best practice:
background-repeat: no-repeat;


CONTAIN
=======
The entire image is visible.

- No cropping
- Empty space may appear

Important behavior:
- Empty space exists
- Image repeats to fill space by default

.box {
    background-size: contain;
}

Always combine with:
background-repeat: no-repeat;


CUSTOM SIZE (PIXELS)
===================
You can set exact width and height.

Important behavior:
- Image is resized
- If smaller than element,
  it repeats by default

.box {
    background-size: 200px 100px;
}

To stop tiling:
background-repeat: no-repeat;


CUSTOM SIZE (PERCENTAGE)
=======================
Size relative to element size.

Important behavior:
- Size depends on element
- Repeats if space exists

.box {
    background-size: 50% 50%;
}


SINGLE VALUE
============
When only one value is given,
it sets the WIDTH.
Height becomes auto.

background-size: 200px;

Result:
- Width → 200px
- Height → auto
- Image repeats if space exists


MULTIPLE BACKGROUND IMAGES
=========================
Each image can have
its own size value.

.layered {
    background-size: cover, contain;
}


IMPORTANT RULE (REMEMBER THIS)
==============================

background-size
→ controls image scaling

background-repeat
→ controls image tiling

They are independent properties.


BEST PRACTICE
=============

Whenever you use background-size,
always decide repetition.

Safe pattern:

.box {
    background-size: cover;   /* or contain */
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
}


COMMON MISTAKES
===============
- Forgetting to set height on element
- Expecting background-size to affect layout
- Thinking contain stops repeating
- Using cover when full image is required


PRACTICE
========
Use one image.
Change background-size values
(auto, cover, contain, px, %)
and observe how the image behaves.

.box {
    height: 250px;
    background-image: url("images/photo.jpg");
    background-size: cover;
}
